    sha256: d9fc5e120f85cb69386c9009d97ed2b33844523429224eded21be3c5003693e2
  body: "# Availability for employment and permitted restrictions\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nThe
    captured JSA guidance distinguishes being available for employment, being treated as available and
    specified joint-claim exemptions, with restrictions considered under their particular rules.\n\n##
    Scope and exceptions\n\nThe selected passage does not make every restriction permissible or require
    unrestricted availability from every claimant. Notice periods, caring, health, treated cases, joint
    claims and hardship have further provisions. Availability and actively seeking employment are separate
    tests; current New Style JSA and UC rules are not inferred.\n\n## Inspected source passages\n\n- [DMG
    21180, PDF page 54](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a8eee245a0c25165ae4658d/dmg-v-4-ch-21.pdf#page=54)
    — DMG Vol 4 Ch 21: Jobseeker’s Allowance Labour market questions, special conditions for JSA(Cont)
    and jobseeking periods.\n- [DMG 21183, PDF page 54](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a8eee245a0c25165ae4658d/dmg-v-4-ch-21.pdf#page=54)
    — DMG Vol 4 Ch 21: Jobseeker’s Allowance Labour market questions, special conditions for JSA(Cont)
    and jobseeking periods.\n- [Inspected continuation, PDF page 158](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a8eee245a0c25165ae4658d/dmg-v-4-ch-21.pdf#page=158)
    — context for the selected paragraph.\n\n## Evidence and review status\n\nModel-assisted interpretation
    of selected passages in the 15 September 2026 frozen source snapshot. Specialist review is required.
    Source acquisition, source revision, legal effective dates and this record’s authoring time are separate.\n\
    \nThis is an independent experimental publication, not an official DWP document, individual advice,
    an entitlement decision or an executable rule. Machine-extracted text may alter reading order, footnotes
    and characters. No PDF visual inspection, whole-chapter coverage or behavioural evaluation pass is
    claimed by this record.\n\n- `dmg-vol4-ch21`: PDF SHA-256 `d9fc5e120f85cb69386c9009d97ed2b33844523429224eded21be3c5003693e2`;
    extracted pages SHA-256 `82d02218371af05595ac356e9c2f48f5434eb15adf97f0d0d057294c33b099c1`.\n"
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/legacy-jsa-actively-seeking
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The chapter expressly distinguishes availability from the activity of seeking work; a
      claimant may be treated as satisfying one without being treated as satisfying the other.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol4-ch21/0158
      quote: "21585 Claimants may be treated, for a particular set of circumstances, as\n\n\n1. ASE but
        not available for employment or\n\n\n2. available for employment but not ASE."
      locator: DMG 21585
  - target: term/legacy-jsa-claimant-commitment
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The agreement-referral passage links the proposed agreement to availability, actively
      seeking employment and reasonableness rather than treating the agreement itself as proof of
      entitlement.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol4-ch21/0205
      quote: "21840 Emp Os may refer a proposed JSAg to a DM to determine whether1\n\n\n1. if claimants
        were to do what is in the proposed JSAg, they would satisfy\n\n\n      1.1 the availability condition
        or\n\n\n      1.2 the ASE condition and\n\n\n2. it is reasonable to expect the claimant to have
        to do what is in the proposed JSAg (see DMG 21850 -\n21851).\n\n\n                           \
        \                                                           1 JS Act 95, s 9(6)"
      locator: DMG 21840
